    <section class="about-section" id="current-work">
      <span class="about-num">01</span>
      <div class="about-section-content">
        <p class="section-kicker">CURRENT WORK</p>
        <h2>From 5G standards to real signals and working code.</h2>
        <p>
          Since 2025, I've worked with the <strong>5G NTN New Radio team at Gatehouse Satcom</strong>,
          developing and testing software for satellite-connected 5G systems.
          Much of my work involves the physical layer (PHY): transmit and receive processing,
          timing and frequency synchronization, channel estimation, demodulation
          and challenges specific to satellite links.
        </p>
        <p>
          What makes this work interesting is connecting different perspectives on the very same signal:
          a 3GPP requirement, a mathematical equation, I/Q samples in memory,
          the processing chain in code, and results from test equipment.
          A concept may look simple on paper yet raise difficult questions in a real implementation.
        </p>
        <div class="about-keywords" aria-label="Technical interests">
          <span>5G NR · PHY</span><span>5G NTN / satellite</span><span>Synchronization</span>
          <span>Channel estimation</span><span>DSP &amp; receiver</span><span>Integration &amp; testing</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section" id="journey">
      <span class="about-num">02</span>
      <div class="about-section-content">
        <p class="section-kicker">EXPERIENCE AND EDUCATION</p>
        <h2>From classrooms and research labs to engineering products.</h2>
        <p>
          I started with electronics and telecommunications in Vietnam, studied further in South Korea,
          carried out research in Luxembourg and pursued my PhD in Denmark.
          I also gained industry experience involving SES and an internship at Bosch in Germany.
        </p>
        <div class="about-timeline">
          <article><time>2013–2018</time><div><h3>Ho Chi Minh City University of Technology · Vietnam</h3><p>BSc in Electronics and Telecommunications. My foundations in signals, systems and communications.</p></div></article>
          <article><time>2018–2020</time><div><h3>Soongsil University · South Korea</h3><p>MSc in Telecommunications, exploring research and analytical approaches to wireless systems.</p></div></article>
          <article><time>2021 and later</time><div><h3>Luxembourg · SnT and SES</h3><p>Research at SnT, University of Luxembourg, and additional experience related to satellite communications and optimization, including work with SES.</p></div></article>
          <article><time>Along the way</time><div><h3>Bosch · Germany</h3><p>An industry internship that broadened my view of engineering beyond academic papers and controlled experiments.</p></div></article>
          <article><time>2022–2026</time><div><h3>Aalborg University · Denmark</h3><p>PhD research on goal-oriented communications, digital twins and 6G systems. I defended my dissertation, <em>“Goal-Oriented Communication in Digital Twins for 6G Wireless Systems”</em>, in 2026.</p></div></article>
        </div>
        <p>
          Each stage has given me another perspective. It has also made me realize that
          <strong>knowing how to use a technique</strong> and <strong>understanding why it works</strong>
          are not necessarily the same.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section" id="questions">
      <span class="about-num">03</span>
      <div class="about-section-content">
        <p class="section-kicker">QUESTIONS I KEEP COMING BACK TO</p>
        <h2>I learned many equations. But where was the bigger picture?</h2>
        <p>
          As a student, I sometimes learned equations to solve assignments without clearly seeing
          what they represented. Later, as a researcher and software developer,
          those basic questions returned. I wanted to answer them through intuition,
          mathematics and experiments instead of memorized definitions.
        </p>
        <div class="about-questions">
          <p><span>01</span> What does an FFT actually do, and why do we move between time and frequency domains?</p>
          <p><span>02</span> How do I/Q, phase, subcarriers and resource grids relate to the RF waveform transmitted by an antenna?</p>
          <p><span>03</span> How do synchronization, channel estimation and demodulation work with multipath, noise and satellite delay?</p>
          <p><span>04</span> How can I understand the whole transmitter–channel–receiver chain rather than isolated blocks?</p>
        </div>
        <blockquote class="about-quote">
          <p>I want to understand a concept well enough to explain it, implement it and verify it—not simply call the right library function.</p>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section" id="notebook">
      <span class="about-num">04</span>
      <div class="about-section-content">
        <p class="section-kicker">WHY VIETWIRELESS EXISTS</p>
        <h2>First of all, it's a notebook for organizing my own understanding.</h2>
        <p>
          One day I might be reading 3GPP; another day I'm debugging a receiver;
          later I open an old textbook to revisit a fundamental idea.
          When I only solve the immediate problem, knowledge remains scattered
          across notes, code and documents. I wanted a place to connect those pieces
          into a coherent learning path I could return to.
        </p>
        <p>
          My approach is to begin with the technical question, build intuition through visual examples,
          introduce the mathematics and its assumptions, test with a numerical example or simulation,
          and connect the result back to code and standards.
          Trying to explain something clearly is often how I discover what I still don't fully understand.
        </p>
        <div class="about-methods">
          <div><strong>Intuition</strong><p>What problem are we solving? Why do we need this idea?</p></div>
          <div><strong>Mathematics</strong><p>What do the variables, transformations and assumptions mean?</p></div>
          <div><strong>Visualization</strong><p>Check our understanding with diagrams, examples and interactive labs.</p></div>
          <div><strong>Implementation</strong><p>Relate each concept to code, signals and real systems.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p class="about-disclaimer">
          These are personal notes, not official materials from any of my past or present employers,
          and not a replacement for textbooks or 3GPP specifications. I'll continue revising them
          whenever I find a clearer way to understand or explain a topic.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

